The task is to find a given value in a Binary Search Tree (BST).
If the value exists, we return the node. If it does not exist, we return null.

I used a while loop to search for the value.
First, I check the root node.
- If the value is equal to the current node, I return it.
- If the value is smaller, I move to the left child.
- If the value is bigger, I move to the right child.
- If the node is null, it means the value was not found.


At first, I needed to understand which direction to choose when searching.
I learned that in a BST, smaller values are on the left and bigger values are on the right. This makes searching easier because I do not need to check every node.
I also considered what happens when the value does not exist. In this case, the loop stops when the current node becomes null.


Time Complexity: O(h)
The algorithm follows only one path from the root to a node. It does not visit the whole tree.
Here, h is the height of the tree.
In the worst case, the tree can be unbalanced, so the complexity becomes O(n).
Space Complexity: O(1)
I only use a few variables and do not create another data structure.

The solution is already efficient because it uses the BST structure.
If the tree is balanced, searching takes O(log n) time.
To improve the worst case, the tree should be balanced. This keeps its height smaller.
